T14.01|Total working capital is current assets less current liabilities. Operating working capital isolates balances tied to the operating cycle, commonly receivables plus inventory and other operating assets less payables and other operating liabilities. Cash and financing debt are often excluded from the operating measure.~~Increasing operating working capital generally uses cash: sales not yet collected and stock not yet sold must be funded. The exact definition depends on the business and model, so state every included balance.|Total working capital: All current assets minus all current liabilities.;;Operating working capital: Selected operating-cycle assets minus liabilities.;;Non-operating items: Cash, borrowing and other balances excluded under a stated model.|Receivables rise ₹8 lakh, inventory rises ₹5 and payables rise ₹4. Assume no other operating balances change.~~Operating assets increase ₹13.;;Supplier funding increases ₹4.;;Net operating working-capital investment = ₹13 − ₹4 = ₹9.~~The business uses ₹9 operating cash before other movements.~~A payable increase is funding from suppliers, not profit; eventual settlement remains an obligation.|List inclusions explicitly.;;Reconcile opening and closing balances.;;Connect each change to cash and operating drivers.
T14.02|The cash conversion cycle estimates how long cash is tied up between paying suppliers and collecting customers. CCC = inventory days + receivable days − payable days under matching conventions. It links procurement, production, sales and collection timing.~~A shorter cycle can release cash but may damage availability or supplier relationships if achieved unsustainably. Negative cycles can be normal for businesses receiving customer cash before paying suppliers.|Inventory stage: Stockholding and production time.;;Receivable stage: Time from sale to collection.;;Payable offset: Supplier credit before payment.|Inventory days are 50, receivable days 30 and payable days 40. Assume a simplified uniform operating cycle.~~Gross operating cycle = 50 + 30 = 80 days.;;Subtract supplier credit: CCC = 80 − 40 = 40 days.;;If receivable days fall to 20, CCC falls to 30 days.~~Faster collection reduces the modelled funding interval by 10 days.~~Translate days to cash using the appropriate sales or cost base; do not multiply every component by revenue indiscriminately.|Use consistent periods and averages.;;Match denominators to balances.;;Evaluate operational trade-offs alongside cash release.
T14.03|Inventory, receivable and payable days translate balances into an approximate operating-cycle duration. Inventory usually uses cost of sales; receivables ideally use relevant credit sales; payables ideally use relevant credit purchases. Available-data approximations must be disclosed.~~Closing balances can be misleading in seasonal businesses. A ratio improvement caused by factoring, an acquisition or a reporting-date payment push is not necessarily an underlying operating improvement.|Inventory days: Average inventory/cost flow × days.;;Receivable days: Average eligible receivables/credit sales × days.;;Payable days: Average trade payables/eligible purchases × days.|Assume annual credit sales ₹365 lakh and average receivables ₹30, using 365 days.~~Receivable days = 30/365 × 365 = 30.;;If average receivables rise to ₹45 with sales unchanged, days become 45.;;The extra ₹15 balance represents additional funds tied in customer credit.~~Longer collection time increases the financing need under these assumptions.~~Disputed balances and credit-loss allowances need separate review.|Use representative averages.;;Identify credit-only denominators where available.;;Investigate collections, terms and balance quality.
T14.04|A cash budget forecasts receipts, payments and funding by date. It differs from a profit budget because taxes, working capital, investment and financing follow cash timing. Short-horizon treasury forecasts often use direct bank-account and transaction information.~~A month-end positive balance can hide a shortage earlier in the month. Forecast timing, uncertainty and committed facilities should be assessed at the granularity needed to meet actual payments.|Direct cash forecast: Expected receipts and payments.;;Indirect forecast: Earnings and balance changes translated to cash.;;Short-horizon forecast: Detailed daily or weekly liquidity planning.|Opening cash is ₹10 lakh. Week one payments are ₹12; week two receipts are ₹8. Assume no other cash flows.~~Week-one closing cash would be −₹2.;;Week-two cumulative closing cash would be ₹6.;;Arrange at least the interim ₹2 funding gap before the first payment deadline.~~A positive two-week closing balance does not remove the earlier shortage.~~Forecast uncertainty may require a larger buffer than the deterministic gap.|Forecast actual settlement dates.;;Reconcile to bank balances.;;Stress receipts, payment priorities and available facilities.
T14.05|Customer credit policy sets eligibility, limits, terms and collection processes. Generous credit can support sales but increases receivables, financing cost and expected loss. A sound decision compares additional contribution with these incremental burdens.~~Collections require monitoring ageing, disputes, promised payments and concentration. High revenue growth with worsening receivable quality may indicate that credit policy is subsidising customers rather than creating sustainable demand.|Credit approval: Assess customer risk before exposure.;;Terms and limits: Control size and repayment dates.;;Collection management: Monitor overdue claims and recover cash.|Longer terms are expected to add ₹10 lakh contribution but require ₹50 extra receivables. Assume annual financing cost 12% and incremental expected losses ₹2.~~Financing burden = ₹50 × 12% = ₹6.;;Net annual contribution after these costs = ₹10 − ₹6 − ₹2 = ₹2.;;Stress delayed collection or higher defaults before approval.~~More sales create only ₹2 net benefit under the simplified assumptions.~~Operating capacity and tax effects may add other incremental costs.|Assess incremental margin, not just sales.;;Model collection and default risk.;;Monitor ageing and concentration after approval.
T14.06|Inventory financing provides cash secured or supported by stock. Lenders assess ownership, valuation, turnover, location and ability to realise collateral. A borrowing base may apply eligibility exclusions and advance-rate discounts.~~Inventory's accounting amount is not necessarily its liquidation value. Obsolescence, price volatility, storage costs and competing claims can reduce recovery and therefore available funding.|Secured inventory loan: Borrowing against eligible stock.;;Warehouse or collateral-controlled finance: Additional control over goods.;;Borrowing base: Eligible value multiplied by an advance rate.|Book inventory is ₹100 lakh; ₹20 is ineligible and the advance rate on eligible stock is 60%.~~Eligible stock = ₹80.;;Maximum modelled borrowing = ₹80 × 60% = ₹48.;;If eligible value falls to ₹60, the base falls to ₹36.~~A ₹100 inventory balance does not support ₹100 funding.~~Existing debt above a reduced base can require repayment or extra collateral under the agreement.|Verify title and eligibility.;;Stress stock values and turnover.;;Read monitoring, margin and collateral-release terms.
T14.07|Supplier terms determine when purchases must be paid and whether discounts are available. Extending payment preserves cash temporarily but may sacrifice discounts, increase prices or damage supply reliability. Trade credit has an economic cost even when no explicit interest line appears.~~Compare the cost of foregoing an early-payment discount with financing alternatives using the actual timing convention. Delaying beyond agreed terms is distinct from negotiating a longer contractual period.|Early-payment discount: Price reduction for prompt payment.;;Contractual credit period: Agreed time to pay.;;Overdue payment: Payment after the agreed deadline.|An invoice offers a 2% discount if paid now; otherwise ₹100 lakh is due in 30 days.~~Early payment is ₹98.;;Waiting effectively uses ₹98 for 30 days at a cost of ₹2.;;Thirty-day cost = 2/98 ≈ 2.04%; a simple 365-day annualisation is about 24.83%.~~A small discount can imply an expensive short-term financing choice.~~Annualisation conventions differ; assess actual liquidity, reliability and funding fees as well.|Read exact dates and discounts.;;Calculate implicit financing cost.;;Protect supplier continuity and agreed terms.
T14.08|Factoring and invoice discounting raise funds against receivables. Arrangements differ in collections responsibility, notice to customers and recourse if the debtor fails. Selling or financing an invoice does not automatically remove it from the balance sheet; derecognition depends on governing criteria and transferred risks.~~Analyse both the liquidity benefit and the all-in cost. Fees, reserves and retained default exposure can make headline advance percentages misleading.|Recourse arrangement: Seller retains specified debtor-loss exposure.;;Non-recourse arrangement: Factor assumes specified risks subject to exclusions.;;Invoice discounting: Funding against receivables under agreed collection terms.|A ₹100 lakh invoice receives an 80% immediate advance, with a ₹3 fee deducted from final proceeds. Assume full customer payment and no other costs.~~Initial cash is ₹80.;;Remaining gross amount is ₹20.;;Final net cash is ₹17, making total receipts ₹97.~~The arrangement accelerates cash but costs ₹3 under these assumptions.~~Accounting and customer-credit exposure depend on the actual agreement, not the product label.|Calculate all-in proceeds and timing.;;Read recourse and exclusions.;;Assess derecognition and customer relationships separately.
T14.09|Reverse factoring or supply-chain finance typically involves a finance provider paying approved supplier invoices, with the buyer paying the provider later. Suppliers may receive earlier cash using the buyer-linked arrangement. The buyer's obligations and payment terms must be analysed transparently.~~Changes can improve reported operating-cycle metrics while introducing financing concentration or withdrawal risk. Classification and disclosure depend on the actual terms and applicable framework.|Buyer-led approval: Buyer validates eligible invoices.;;Supplier acceleration: Supplier receives payment before original maturity.;;Buyer funding extension: Buyer may pay the provider at a later date.|A supplier's ₹100 lakh invoice originally matures in 30 days. A provider pays ₹98 now and the buyer pays ₹100 in 60 days.~~Supplier exchanges waiting for an early ₹98 receipt.;;Buyer receives 30 extra days relative to the original due date.;;Identify the financing provider's credit exposure and the consequences if the programme ends.~~Both parties' cash timing changes, but the buyer still owes ₹100.~~Assess whether the obligation is effectively borrowing and what disclosures are required; the label alone does not decide.|Read original and revised terms.;;Identify who bears loss and funding risk.;;Stress programme withdrawal and classification.
T14.10|Cash-credit, overdraft and revolving facilities provide flexible short-term borrowing under different contractual structures. Available liquidity equals usable undrawn capacity, not merely the headline commitment. Covenants, collateral, borrowing bases and expiry dates can restrict access.~~A revolving line can support seasonal needs, but persistent full utilisation may indicate a structural funding mismatch. Uncommitted facilities may be withdrawn under their terms and are weaker contingency support than dependable commitments.|Overdraft: Account-linked borrowing within agreed terms.;;Revolver: Draw, repay and redraw within a commitment.;;Borrowing-base facility: Availability linked to eligible collateral.|A ₹50 lakh facility has ₹30 drawn, but a borrowing-base restriction permits only ₹40 total drawings.~~Headline undrawn amount is ₹20.;;Usable undrawn amount is ₹40 − ₹30 = ₹10.;;Combine the ₹10 with accessible cash when planning payments.~~The contractual limit and presently usable limit can differ.~~Fees, maturity and covenant tests may further affect availability.|Confirm committed and usable capacity.;;Read conditions and expiry.;;Match temporary borrowing with realistic repayment cash.
T14.11|Commercial paper and other short-term funding rely on investor appetite and rollover access. They can offer efficient funding to eligible issuers but create refinancing risk when liabilities mature before underlying assets or cash generation. Backup liquidity must be assessed under stress.~~A lower short-term rate should not be compared with long-term borrowing without considering maturity risk and facility fees. Markets can close precisely when the issuer's operating cash weakens.|Commercial paper: Short-dated market-issued debt under applicable rules.;;Rollover funding: New issuance replaces maturing debt.;;Committed backup: Contracted liquidity supporting failed rollover.|₹100 lakh commercial paper matures next week. Expected operating receipts are ₹30 and usable committed backup is ₹50.~~Cash resources cover ₹80.;;Uncovered maturity need is ₹20 before any new issuance.;;Test whether refinancing remains feasible in a stressed market.~~Cheap funding still carries a ₹20 uncovered rollover gap in this case.~~Eligibility, tenure and issuance rules are jurisdiction-specific; the example addresses economic mechanics.|Map maturity concentration.;;Verify backup access and conditions.;;Stress simultaneous market closure and weak receipts.
T14.12|Cash pooling coordinates balances across group entities. Physical pooling transfers funds between accounts; notional arrangements offset balances for specified banking purposes without the same transfer pattern. Intercompany funding creates claims that require documentation and legal, tax and credit assessment.~~Group cash is not automatically freely movable. Minority rights, insolvency rules, capital controls, currency restrictions and bank agreements can limit pooling and guarantees.|Physical pooling: Actual movement of cash balances.;;Notional pooling: Contractual aggregation for specified purposes.;;Intercompany loan: Documented funding claim between entities.|Subsidiary A has ₹20 lakh excess cash and B needs ₹15. Assume lawful, approved physical transfer with a documented loan.~~Transfer ₹15 from A to B.;;A records an intercompany receivable and B a payable.;;Group cash remains unchanged before fees, while its location changes.~~Pooling reallocates liquidity within the group rather than creating cash.~~Consolidation eliminates internal claims, but legal-entity repayment and restrictions remain relevant.|Assess entity-level restrictions.;;Document transfers and pricing.;;Review guarantees, netting and insolvency exposure.
T14.13|Treasury investment policy governs placement of cash that must remain available for operations. Safety, liquidity and return are ordered according to purpose; a reserve needed next month should not be treated like a long-horizon equity portfolio.~~Policies specify eligible instruments, maturities, counterparties, concentration limits and monitoring. A highly liquid market security can still lose value, and a high quoted yield may reflect credit or access risk.|Operating cash: Immediate payment needs.;;Liquidity reserve: Contingency resources with dependable access.;;Strategic surplus: Longer-horizon funds under separately approved objectives.|A firm needs ₹10 lakh in one month and has ₹15 cash. It considers investing all ₹15 in volatile shares.~~Identify the ₹10 near-term liability.;;Match that requirement with appropriately accessible low-risk resources.;;Evaluate the remaining ₹5 only under the approved surplus policy.~~Payment-purpose cash and return-seeking capital require different mandates.~~Eligibility and capital preservation cannot be inferred solely from an investment's ability to be sold.|Segment cash by purpose.;;Set maturity and concentration limits.;;Monitor counterparty risk and actual access.
T14.14|Treasury exposure includes foreign-currency receipts and payments, floating interest, refinancing and asset-liability mismatches. Netting natural offsets reduces the exposure before derivatives are considered. A hedge should fit quantity, date and risk driver.~~Fixed debt reduces rate-reset risk but can carry refinancing or market-value risk; floating debt changes cash interest with benchmark rates. Derivatives add basis, collateral and counterparty considerations.|Transaction FX exposure: Contractual foreign-currency cash flows.;;Interest-rate exposure: Cash or value sensitivity to rate changes.;;Natural versus derivative hedge: Operational offset or financial contract.|A firm expects $100,000 customer receipts and $70,000 supplier payments on the same date. Assume no other currency flows.~~Natural net receipt = $30,000.;;A hedge of $100,000 would ignore the supplier offset.;;Assess hedging the $30,000 net exposure, subject to timing and forecast reliability.~~Gross and net currency exposures differ.~~If payment dates or currencies differ, the offset may be incomplete and liquidity still matters.|Map gross flows and dates.;;Net only genuine matching offsets.;;Assess residual risk, collateral and counterparty terms.
T14.15|A liquidity buffer is accessible funding reserved for unexpected needs. Contingency planning identifies stress events, funding sources, triggers and actions. Cash, readily realisable assets and dependable facilities differ in access speed and conditions.~~Buffers should be sized against plausible combined shocks rather than one average payment gap. Counting the same pledged assets as both sale proceeds and facility collateral can double-count liquidity.|Cash buffer: Immediately usable balances.;;Asset liquidity: Realisable proceeds with timing and haircuts.;;Funding reserve: Usable committed borrowing capacity.|Available cash is ₹8 lakh and usable committed borrowing ₹12. A stress scenario needs ₹25 before uncertain customer receipts.~~Dependable resources total ₹20.;;Stress funding gap = ₹25 − ₹20 = ₹5.;;Identify feasible additional funding or payment reductions before the scenario occurs.~~The stated buffer is insufficient for the selected stress.~~A larger nominal facility may not help if its access conditions fail during the same stress.|Define simultaneous stress needs.;;Count only accessible resources once.;;Set triggers, owners and feasible contingency actions.
T14.16|Treasury controls protect cash and reduce errors, unauthorised transfers and counterparty concentration. Core controls include segregation of duties, verified instructions, dual approval, reconciliation and limit monitoring. Controls should address the full transaction lifecycle rather than only payment release.~~A profitable or reputable counterparty can still fail or present operational risk. Exposure aggregation includes deposits, derivatives, guarantees and unsettled payments under the chosen risk definitions.|Preventive control: Approval and access restrictions before execution.;;Detective control: Reconciliation and exception review.;;Counterparty limit: Bound aggregate exposure by institution or group.|A supplier requests a bank-account change by email immediately before a ₹20 lakh payment.~~Verify the change through an independently established contact channel.;;Require separate approval of master-data change and payment.;;Reconcile bank confirmation and investigate exceptions after execution.~~Independent checks reduce the chance that one compromised instruction releases funds.~~A control is effective only if staff cannot routinely bypass it and exceptions are reviewed.|Separate initiation, approval and reconciliation.;;Verify account changes independently.;;Aggregate and monitor counterparty exposures.
